// Handyboard intrinsics

// RESERVED WORDS

// KEYWORD #define

<pre>#define macro expression</pre><br>

The #define command creates textual replacements in C.  For example,
if you were to have the following:<br><br>

<pre>#define PI 3.14159</pre>

then any time PI occurs in your program, it will be replaced by
3.14159.  #define does not affect text within quotes, and only
replaces whole words, so the following would not be affected by the
above define:<br><br>

<pre>SPIED
"I love PI"</pre>

A #define statement only affects the remainder of the file after the
#define.<br><br>

Your #define macro can also take arguments.  Here's an example:<br><br>

<pre>#define fdslow(x) motor(x,50)</pre>

This example would replace fdslow(1) with motor(1,50)

