// KEYWORD break

<pre>break;<pre>

The break statement causes the enclosing for or while loop to stop
immediately, and *for execution to resume at the next statement
following the loop.<br><br>

The break statement is only valid inside a *for* or *while* loop.<br><br>

A simple example, which waits until the start button is pressed, then
beeps:<br><br>

<pre>
while (1) {
    if (start_button()) { 
        // Start button got pressed
        // Stop the loop, go straight to first statement after loop
        break; 
    }
}
beep(); // first statement after loop
</pre>

If break is used within nested loops, it only causes the innermost
loop to exit.  This example program tests four motors sequentially,
until the stop button is pressed:

<pre>

int m;
// Test each motor, one at a time, until the stop button is pressed
// When done, beep
while (1) {
    for (m= 0; m <= 3; m++) {
        fd(m);
        sleep(0.5);
        if (stop_button()) break;
        bk(m);
        sleep(0.5);
        if (stop_button()) break;
        off(m)
    }
    // The break statements inside the for loop only exit that loop,
    // We need to check again and call break from here to leave the while loop
    if (stop_button()) break;
}
alloff(); 
beep();   // done!
</pre>

See also: for, while, continue

// KEYWORD continue

<pre>
continue;
</pre>

The continue statement causes the enclosing for or while loop to skip
the rest of the loop body for the current iteration.  Unlike break,
continue does not stop the loop.  Instead, if the loop still wants to
execute (the while or for condition is true), the loop will continue
with the next iteration.<br><br>

Example:<br>

<pre>
// Test each motor, but skip motor 2
int m;
for (m= 0; m <= 3; m++) {
    if (m == 2) continue; // skip to next iteration
    fd(m);
    sleep(0.5);
    bk(m);
    sleep(0.5);
    off(m);
}

// KEYWORD char
<pre>
char foo[4];
char foo[4]="abc";
</pre>

The char type represents a number from 0 to 255, and can be used to
represent a single text character using ASCII encoding.  In
Interactive C, the char type is only used in arrays, typically for
strings.  If you want to express a single number, use an int or a long
instead.

// LINK ASCII

